 "standard_text": "Add to 99 and subtract from 99; use techniques such as building up and breaking down numbers, doubling and halving, number lines, and rounding off in tens.",
 "meta_desc": "Free CAPS Grade 2 maths worksheets: adding and subtracting two-digit numbers to 99, with and without bridging, and missing numbers. 100 questions with memos.",
 "key_big": "Add the tens, then the ones.",
 "key_sub": "Check by adding back.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Break up the numbers: 34 + 25 = 30 + 20 + 4 + 5.</li><li>Add the tens, add the ones, then put them together.</li><li>If the ones make 10 or more, make a new ten.</li><li>Check a subtraction by adding.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>47 + 25</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>40 + 20 = 60</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>7 + 5 = 12</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>60 + 12</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 72</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Forgetting the ten</td><td>12 ones = 1 ten and 2 ones.</td></tr><tr><td>Bigger minus smaller</td><td>52 − 27: you cannot take 7 from 2, so break a ten.</td></tr><tr><td>Tens twice</td><td>Add the tens once.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is the Foundation Phase?",
   "a": "Grade R to Grade 3 in South African schools. Learners are about 7 to 8 years old in Grade 2."
